#N canvas 0 0 700 500 10;
#X obj 20 20 inlet;
#X text 110 20 one sounding voice \, wrapping a play_loop~ pointed at a loadable array. inlet accepts either bang (stop: 5ms fade to silence) or a 4-element list "rate startSamples endSamples arrayName" (play: re-points play_loop~ at arrayName \, sets its loop params \, retriggers from loop start \, and 5ms-fades in). creation arg: default array name to point at before the first real message arrives (callers should always send a real one before expecting sound).;
#X obj 20 60 route bang;
#X msg 20 100 0 5;
#X obj 150 100 unpack f f f s;
#X obj 300 140 symbol;
#X msg 300 180 set \$1;
#X obj 150 180 pack f f f;
#X obj 150 220 t b b l;
#X msg 150 260 1 5;
#X obj 20 300 line~;
#X obj 20 340 play_loop~ \$1;
#X obj 20 380 *~;
#X obj 20 420 outlet~;
#X text 300 220 turn 10 (2026-09-18): pack here is float-only (rate/end/start) \, so plain numeric creation args are fine -- the "arrayname" element travels on its OWN branch (unpack outlet3 -> symbol -> msg set \$1) straight into play_loop~ \, never through pack. An earlier probe found that mixing a symbol into a [pack f f f <name>] creation arg is invalid syntax -- Pd interprets an unrecognized word as a pointer-type inlet \, not a symbol default \, and errors "consistency check failed: gpointer_copy" the first time a real symbol arrives. The fix \, if a symbol slot in a pack is ever needed here later \, is the literal type keyword 's' \, e.g. [pack f f f s] -- never a sample value.;
#X connect 0 0 2 0;
#X connect 2 0 3 0;
#X connect 3 0 10 0;
#X connect 2 1 4 0;
#X connect 4 3 5 0;
#X connect 5 0 6 0;
#X connect 6 0 11 0;
#X connect 4 2 7 1;
#X connect 4 1 7 2;
#X connect 4 0 7 0;
#X connect 7 0 8 0;
#X connect 8 2 11 0;
#X connect 8 1 11 0;
#X connect 8 0 9 0;
#X connect 9 0 10 0;
#X connect 10 0 12 1;
#X connect 11 0 12 0;
#X connect 12 0 13 0;
